{"move_object":{"move":"TRY","confusion":"Believes that touching two fields of a struct makes the closure capture the whole struct by mutable borrow, so any later use of either field fails.","confusion_skill":"F4","entity_id":862,"note":"They still think two field captures = whole-struct capture. The TRY should show the compiler only complains about the field the closure actually borrows (hits), not label. Next turn: check they read the error and can say why line 39 is fine.","response":"Not quite. The compiler looks at the exact paths the body names, not at how many fields the struct has. `stats.hits` and `stats.label` are two different places in memory, so borrowing one does not block the other.

Try it. In `capture.rs`, change line 37 to:

```rust
let mut hit = || { stats.hits += 1; println!("label inside: {}", stats.label); };
```

Then run it. Which line does the compiler complain about, and what does that tell you about what `hit` actually borrows?","suggestion":"Change line 37 to `let mut hit = || { stats.hits += 1; println!("label inside: {}", stats.label); };` and run. Read the error.","topic":"field-level capture"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks a closure that captures a borrowed `&str` can be stored in a `Box<dyn Fn>` field, because the code compiled and the tests passed.","confusion_skill":"F4","highlights":[{"entity_id":906,"end_line":14,"path":"","start_line":12},{"entity_id":906,"end_line":20,"path":"","start_line":18},{"entity_id":906,"end_line":59,"path":"","start_line":52}],"note":"They pasted ex2_rules.rs with `without` capturing `word` directly, and reported the run as passing. The pasted stdout shows test names (keeps_the_same_buffer, basic, empty) that are not in ex2_rules.rs, so the run may not be of this file. Next: get them to re-run ex2_rules.rs and read the lifetime error, then fix `without` by owning a copy of the word. Also check whether they notice the missing semicolon on line 9.","response":"Look at line 9 first: `type Rule = Box<dyn Fn(&str) -> bool>` has no semicolon. That alone stops the file compiling, so the run you saw may not be this file. The test names in your output (keeps_the_same_buffer, basic, empty) are not in ex2_rules.rs, so re-run it and read the real error.

Now the real question. In `without`, the closure names `word`, which is a `&str` borrowed from the caller. A `Box<dyn Fn>` field is short for `Box<dyn Fn + 'static>`, so the closure must own everything it uses. Does `word` own anything? What does the closure hold instead?

Try it: run `rustc --edition 2024 --test -o main ex2_rules.rs && ./main --test-threads=4` and tell me the first error you get.","topic":"Boxed closure must own its captures"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"TRY","confusion":"Believes `println!` of an adapter chain runs the closure, so `println!` is a consumer.","confusion_skill":"F5","entity_id":868,"note":"They restated the old laziness error a third time, now asking why println! is not a consumer. Had them run the one-line test in iterators.rs. Next turn: check they can say what actually pulled items (collect on line 26), and that println! only formats the Map value.","response":"Let's find out by running it. In `iterators.rs`, right after line 25, add one line that prints the adapter itself, before the `collect` on line 26:

```rust
println!("adapter: {:?}", scores.iter().map(|s| s * 2));
```

Before you run it, write down two things: what you expect to see on screen, and whether the "map sees" lines appear. Then run it and tell me what actually showed up.","suggestion":"After line 25, add: `println!("adapter: {:?}", scores.iter().map(|s| s * 2));`","topic":"println! is not a consumer"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"CHECK_ME","confusion":"Believes a closure that mutates a captured variable is still `Fn` because `move` gives it ownership, so it would fit `Box<dyn Fn>`.","confusion_skill":"F4","note":"They answered the Fn vs FnMut question wrong: they think `move` makes a mutating closure `Fn`. Checking whether they can say that `Fn` requires `&self` (no mutation) and that a closure with `seen += 1` is `FnMut`, so `Box<dyn Fn>` rejects it. If they get it, they are ready for the check.","response":"`move` sirf ownership deta hai, mutation ki permission nahi. `Fn` ka matlab hai closure ko `&self` se call kiya ja sakta hai, aur `&self` se aap captured value ko badal nahi sakte. `FnMut` ka matlab hai `&mut self` se call, jahan mutation allowed hai.

To socho: `seen += 1` ke liye closure ko `seen` ko badalna padta hai. Uske liye kaunsa receiver chahiye, `&self` ya `&mut self`? Aur `Box<dyn Fn(&str) -> bool>` ka `Fn` kaunsa receiver maangta hai?

Jawab do, phir batao ki aisa closure `Vec<Box<dyn Fn(&str) -> bool>>` me store ho sakta hai ya nahi.","topic":"Fn vs FnMut for mutating closures"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"REDIRECT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"Asked for exercise solutions and assessment questions/answers. Refused and steered back to the lesson. Next turn: check whether they can start exercise 1 (ex1_zoo.rs) by predicting the default speak output for Dog and Cat.","response":"I can't do either of those. I don't write out exercise solutions, and I don't know what the assessment asks, so I can't give you answers to it. What I can do is get you unstuck on the exercise itself.

Open `ex1_zoo.rs` and look at the `Animal` trait. `speak` has a default body that calls `self.name()` and `self.sound()`. The test on line 77 expects `Dog.speak()` to be `"dog says woof"`.

So: what should `Dog`'s `name()` return, and what should its `sound()` return? Answer those two and the first `impl` block is done. Tell me what you think and we'll go from there."}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks `run_for` should add up the length of the colour name rather than the light's `seconds()`.","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":907,"end_line":16,"path":"","start_line":13},{"entity_id":907,"end_line":75,"path":"","start_line":71}],"note":"They pasted a full ex3 solution that looks correct except run_for uses self.color().len() as u32 instead of seconds(). The build output they showed (E0507 on `name[0]` in a Vec<String>) does not match their pasted file, so they may have run a different version. Next turn: confirm they fixed run_for and that the build error is gone, then check whether they can explain why Yellow's seconds is 5 while the others use the default.","response":"Your `next`, `color` and `advance` all look right, and `take()` is the right way to get the owned box out of the `Option`. So let's look at `run_for`.

The doc comment on line 71 says the total is the sum of the lights' seconds, and the test on line 103 expects `run_for(3)` to be 65. Your loop adds `self.color().len() as u32` instead.

Before you change anything: for a signal starting at red, what does your loop add on the first pass, and what does the test expect for that first pass?","topic":"run_for should sum seconds"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":904,"end_line":20,"path":"","start_line":14},{"entity_id":904,"end_line":36,"path":"","start_line":32}],"note":"They asked why the error appears at the dyn Shape use site rather than at the method definition, and guessed the vtable needs a fixed return type. I am not confirming or denying that guess; I am asking them to reason from the two highlighted lines: the trait compiles on its own, so what does the compiler need to know at line 32 that it does not need at line 14? Checking they can say the vtable is only built where dyn Shape is requested, and that the return type is the problem because the caller must reserve space for Self whose size is unknown behind dyn.","response":"Good question, and you are close. Look at the two highlighted lines. Line 14 is just a trait definition, and line 32 is where you actually ask for a `dyn Shape`.

So: what does the compiler have to know at line 32 that it does not have to know at line 14? And what does it have to build at line 32 that it does not build at line 14?

Answer those two and you will have the reason yourself. Then run it with both `doubled` lines uncommented and tell me the line the compiler points at.","topic":"Why dyn Shape fails at use"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"EXPLAIN","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"They asked why a move-but-only-reads closure is Fn, not FnOnce. Explained that FnOnce is about the body moving the capture out, and that a move closure that only reads still has its capture intact after a call, so &self works. Next: check they can say what the body would have to do to make it FnOnce, and whether they can now confirm the capture.rs part 3 experiment.","response":"The trait is decided by what the body does to the capture, not by how the capture got there. `FnOnce` is the trait for a body that moves its capture out, so after one call the capture is gone and there is nothing left to call again. A `move` closure that only reads a `String` never moves it out: it still owns the string after the call, so a second call through `&self` is fine. `move` only changed where the string lives (owned by the closure, not borrowed from the outside), it did not change what the body does with it.

So the question to ask of any closure is: does the body take the capture out of the closure? If yes, `FnOnce`. If it only reads, `Fn`.

Now, in `capture.rs` part 3, what would the body have to do to make that closure `FnOnce` instead?","topic":"move does not force FnOnce"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Believes closures always capture the whole struct by value, even in edition 2021, and that `move` is the same as a normal closure.","confusion_skill":"F4","highlights":[{"entity_id":862,"end_line":41,"path":"","start_line":35}],"note":"They hold two wrong beliefs: whole-struct capture in 2021, and `move` being equivalent to a normal closure. Pointing at capture.rs part 4 should let them see the field-level capture and the `move` vs borrow difference themselves. Next turn: check whether they can say which fields are borrowed and what `move` changes.","response":"Not quite, and the file in front of you settles it. Look at lines 35 to 41 of `capture.rs`.

Line 37 is `let mut hit = || stats.hits += 1;`. The body only touches `stats.hits`. Line 39 then reads `stats.label`. If the closure had captured the whole `stats` struct, that line would be an error. Run it and see what happens.

So: which fields of `stats` does that closure actually capture, and what does that leave free for line 39?","topic":"field-level capture in 2021"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":907,"end_line":98,"path":"","start_line":94}],"note":"Their ex3_signal.rs code is correct: Red/Green/Yellow impls, Signal::new, advance with take(), run_for adding seconds then advancing. The run output they pasted (tests::basic, keeps_the_same_buffer, empty) does not match ex3_signal.rs's three tests, so it looks like a stale binary. Next: check they can say why Yellow's seconds() is 5 while Red/Green use the default 30, and that the default body is compiled per type.","response":"Your code looks right to me. `advance` takes the box out with `take()`, calls `next` on the owned box, and puts the new one back. `run_for` adds the current light's seconds and then moves on, so the signal ends up `n` lights further on. That matches the tests.

One thing: the run output you pasted lists `tests::basic`, `tests::keeps_the_same_buffer` and `tests::empty`. `ex3_signal.rs` has `cycle_of_colours`, `default_and_overridden_seconds` and `run_for_adds_up_and_moves_on`. So that output is from a different file. Re-run the command at the top of `ex3_signal.rs` and tell me what you get.

While you do that, look at lines 94 to 98. `Red.seconds()` and `Green.seconds()` both pass, but `Yellow.seconds()` is 5. `Yellow` never writes a `seconds` body. So where does that 5 come from, and why do the other two get 30?","topic":"Yellow's overridden seconds"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":868,"end_line":61,"path":"","start_line":59}],"note":"They asked what the Result collect row means and want an example. Pointed at adapters.rs lines 59-61 (good vs bad). Next turn: check they can say the bad collect stops at the first Err and returns it, and that the good one builds Ok(vec). Then move to F4 (Fn/FnMut/FnOnce, move) since shout_all edit is still unrun.","response":"Look at `adapters.rs` lines 59 to 61. Line 59 builds `good`, line 60 builds `bad`.

Before you run it, predict two things: what does `good` hold, and what does `bad` hold? Then run it and tell me the two lines you see.

The row you quoted is saying the same thing: `collect` on an iterator of `Result`s either gives you `Ok(vec)` with all the values, or it stops at the first `Err` and hands that `Err` back instead. Nothing after the first `Err` is ever looked at.","topic":"collect on Results"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":862,"end_line":33,"path":"","start_line":29}],"note":"They correctly explained shared borrow for read-only and mutable borrow for assignment. Next: part 3 of capture.rs, where `move` forces by-value capture even though the body only reads. Check whether they can say the closure owns `label` and the outer `label` is moved, and whether they connect this to the E0382 error on the uncommented line.","response":"Exactly right. Read only means the weakest capture is a shared borrow, and `count += 1` needs a mutable borrow, so `&mut count`.

Now look at part 3 of `capture.rs`, lines 29 to 33. The body only reads `label`, so without `move` it would be a shared borrow. But the line says `move || println!("owned: {label}")`.

What does `move` change about how `label` is stored in the closure, and what does that mean for the `println!` on line 33?","topic":"move forces by-value capture"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"PROBE","asked":"Read the tkprof: what does the file tell you, and what would you change?","last_stage_performance":"Named the per-row SELECT SUM and the GL_CODE_COMBINATIONS lookup as the dominant hotspots, correctly framed as an N+1 pattern, and proposed the right shape of fix: pre-aggregate the AP distributions, join GL_CODE_COMBINATIONS instead of looking it up per row, push the expense-account filter into SQL, and rewrite the cursor as one set-based INSERT ... SELECT. Also flagged the 42-minute pre-start delay as a separate concurrent-manager thread. Did not read the actual counts out of the file, saying they were not confident enough to quote them, and declined to do the per-execution arithmetic, though they described the method. Did name the MO_GLOB_ORG_ACCESS_TMP line as the multi-org security predicate and correctly said it is why the statement is constrained by EBS security context rather than by the invoice table alone, but did not say what it costs per execution or that it appears in the plan of both the driving query and the per-row sum.","note":"Moving to the set-based rewrite and the India accrual anomaly. Want to see whether they can explain why the rewrite's August output is low against the controller's figure, and whether they connect it to the Accounting Flexfield structures and the SEGMENT3 expense-account filter rather than to the rewrite itself.","probe_id":1,"response":"Fair enough on the counts, and you did name the line I was pointing at, so let us leave the arithmetic there.

The team took your shape of fix and rewrote the program as one set-based statement. It runs in six minutes and its output matches the old program exactly for June and July. For August it does not. The India controller says India's August accrual should be about ₹5.1 crore. The rewrite shows ₹1.92 crore. Here is the rewrite's August output summary, and the two Accounting Flexfield structures the program works against.

Why is India low, and why did the parity check not catch it?"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks a default method's body is always the trait's own code, so overriding it would not change what a dyn call does.","confusion_skill":"D2","highlights":[{"entity_id":911,"end_line":15,"path":"","start_line":12},{"entity_id":911,"end_line":33,"path":"","start_line":30}],"note":"They believe default methods always run the trait's own body, so overriding would not affect a dyn call. Pointed at the vtable diagram and the two label rows. Next: check they say Yellow's vtable row points at its own seconds body, so dyn calls get 5, and that the default body is compiled per type.","response":"Not quite. A default method is not one piece of code that every type shares. The compiler makes a separate copy of it for each type that implements the trait, and each copy is compiled with that type's `self`.

Look at the diagram: `Text`'s `label` row points at the default body compiled for `Text`, and `Checkbox`'s `label` row points at `Checkbox::label`. Two different rows, two different functions.

So for `Yellow`, which `seconds` body does its vtable row point at, and what does that mean for a call through `dyn Light`?","topic":"default methods and vtables"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":911,"end_line":15,"path":"","start_line":9},{"entity_id":911,"end_line":33,"path":"","start_line":26}],"note":"They asked how a default method body is compiled per implementing type and how Text and Checkbox get different vtable entries. Pointed at the trait and the two impl blocks; next check they can say the default body is compiled once per type with that type's Self, so Text's row points at the default compiled for Text and Checkbox's row points at its override.","response":"A default method is not one function. It is a template the compiler fills in for each type that implements the trait, with that type's `Self` in place. So `Text` gets a copy of the default body where `self` is a `&Text`, and `Checkbox` gets its own override where `self` is a `&Checkbox`.

Look at the trait (lines 9 to 15) and then the two impl blocks (lines 20 to 24 and 26 to 33). `Text` never writes a `label`, so it inherits the default. `Checkbox` writes one.

So: how many distinct `label` functions exist after the compiler is done, and which one does each type's vtable row point at?","topic":"default methods per type"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"PROBE","asked":"With fix1 applied, 25.5% of frames still fail and only words 0 to 7 are wrong, plus three rmmod warnings. Why does the 25.5% survive, pointing at the lines in cap_complete() that cause it, and what is each warning telling them?","last_stage_performance":"They got there, but only after being told the sync span and ordering were fine. They named the four header writes above the sync as the thing that dirties the first line, and correctly said the sync then has to deal with a line that holds both CPU-written header bytes and the first 32 bytes of payload, which is why only words 0 to 7 are wrong. They did not name arm64's handling of a partly covered first line, and their fix was to avoid CPU stores into the shared line or move the header out, which is the right direction but they did not say what the sync actually does to that line. On the warnings they were solid: the map error is an unchecked dma_map_single, the fclk0 warnings are a double disable from devm_clk_get_enabled plus cap_remove, and the DMA debug warning is teardown while DMA is still active. They did not mention the devm IRQ still being live when buffers are freed.","note":"Next stage is the soak and the logic analyzer capture. I want to see whether they connect the descriptor error to the CPU's writes to the descriptor memory not being ordered against the DESC_TAIL write, and whether they name writel_relaxed() in cap_queue_buf() as the cause. Then whether they can say what writel() does on arm64 that writel_relaxed() does not, and whether dma_wmb() before writel_relaxed() would be enough.","probe_id":1,"response":"That is it. The header writes dirty the line, and the sync has to deal with a line that is no longer just device data. Let me move you on.

The team moved the header fields out of the buffers, so the first line is now just payload. They ran a 40-minute soak with stress-ng hammering DDR, and the block started losing frames. The logic analyzer caught the fourth error. cap_queue_buf() is highlighted in the capture.

What happened on the bus, which line of the driver causes it, and what is the fix?"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","highlights":[{"entity_id":865,"end_line":36,"path":"","start_line":32},{"entity_id":865,"end_line":59,"path":"","start_line":58}],"note":"They asked how the iterator decides which numbers to include, so they may not yet see that take_while is the gatekeeper and filter is only the even test. Checking whether they can name the stopping condition and the even test separately.","response":"Yes, 0 + 2 + 8 = 10. The question is which numbers get a chance to be summed at all.

