{"move_object": {"move": "EXPLAIN", "confusion": "", "confusion_skill": "", "note": "They asked for the rule that separates capture style from trait choice, and want one small example. Explained: trait is decided by what the body does with the capture (read / change / move out), move only changes how the capture is stored. Next turn: check they can apply it to fn_traits.rs lines 41-45 (move out of words -> FnOnce) and to the make_adder closure (only reads -> Fn even with move).", "response": "The trait is decided by what the body does with the capture, not by how the capture got there. `move` only changes how the capture is stored, owned instead of borrowed. The body then decides the trait: reading the capture gives `Fn`, changing it gives `FnMut`, moving it out gives `FnOnce`.

Small example. `let n = 5; let c = move || n;` is `Fn`, because the body only reads `n`. `let mut n = 5; let c = move || n += 1;` is `FnMut`, because the body changes `n`. `let n = 5; let c = move || { let x = n; x };` is `FnOnce`, because the body moves `n` out.

So `move` is about storage, the body is about the trait. Now apply it: in `fn_traits.rs` lines 41 to 45, what does the body do with `words`, and what trait does that give?", "topic": "trait vs capture style"}}

{"move_object": {"move": "TRY", "confusion": "Thinks `dyn Trait` is a normal sized type that can be stored directly in a `Vec` or returned by value, with the pointer being only an optimisation.", "confusion_skill": "D4", "entity_id": 910, "note": "They believe dyn Trait is sized and the pointer is optional. The TRY should show the compiler rejecting a bare `dyn Display` in a Vec slot, and the size_of line should show 16 bytes. Next turn: check whether they can say why the size is unknown (no single size for all implementors) and whether they connect that to the fat pointer.", "response": "Not an optimisation. `dyn Trait` is an unsized type, so it has no size the compiler can write down. That is why it can never sit in a `Vec` slot or be returned by value: a slot needs a fixed width, and a value return needs a fixed width too.

You can see the compiler enforce it. In `static_vs_dyn.rs`, line 28 builds a `Vec<Box<dyn Display>>`. Try changing that line to `let mixed: Vec<dyn Display> = vec![Box::new(7), Box::new("seven")];` and run it. Read the error, then tell me what the compiler says is missing.

While you are there, line 35 prints the sizes. What does it print for `&dyn Display` compared with `&i32`, and what does that tell you about the pointer?", "suggestion": "In `static_vs_dyn.rs` line 28, change `let mixed: Vec<Box<dyn Display>> = vec![Box::new(7), Box::new("seven"), Box::new(7.5)];` to `let mixed: Vec<dyn Display> = vec![Box::new(7), Box::new("seven")];` and run it. Read the error, then look at line 35's size output.", "topic": "dyn Trait is unsized"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"REDIRECT","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"Learner asked for reference solutions, assessment answers, tutor notes and the rubric. Refused and steered back to the lesson. Next turn: if they engage, ask them to answer one of the section 10 check-yourself questions (e.g. why dyn Trait must sit behind a pointer) or to re-run one of the runnable files.","response":"I can't do that. I don't have the assessment questions, and I won't share reference solutions, notes or a rubric. What I can do is help you work through the lesson, which is where the answers come from.

You have all 18 steps ticked, so you are in good shape. Section 10, Check yourself, has five questions. Pick one and answer it in your own words, then tell me what you said and I will tell you if it holds up.

For example: why must `dyn Trait` always sit behind a pointer, and what are the two halves of that pointer? Have a go at that one and I will take it from there."}}

{"move_object":{"move":"EXPLAIN","confusion":"Believes `dyn Trait` is a normal sized value that can live directly in a `Vec` or on the stack, with the pointer only for convenience.","confusion_skill":"D4","note":"They think dyn Trait is sized and can live inline. Next turn: check whether they can say why the compiler cannot know the size of a trait object, and whether they can point at the fat pointer in widgets.rs or static_vs_dyn.rs.","response":"That is the opposite of what the lesson says, and it is worth getting straight. `dyn Trait` is unsized: its size is not known at compile time, because a `Text` and a `Checkbox` are different sizes. The compiler cannot reserve a slot in a `Vec` or on the stack for something whose size it does not know. That is why a trait object always sits behind a pointer, and the pointer is not for convenience, it is the only way to store it.

The vtable is not stored inside the value. It is a separate table the compiler builds for each (concrete type, trait) pair, and the pointer holds a reference to it. So a call does not jump straight to the method: it follows the vtable pointer, reads the row, then calls the function found there. That is the indirect call, and it is the cost of dynamic dispatch.

Look at `static_vs_dyn.rs` line 35. What does `size_of::<&dyn Display>()` print, and what does that tell you about how big the pointer is?","topic":"dyn Trait is unsized"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"EXPLAIN","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"Learner asked what 'look again at 10. Check yourself' means. Explained that section 10 is a self-check, not a code run, and gave the first question with a pointer to widgets.rs lines 12-14 and 30-32. Next turn: see whether they can say which label each type prints and why, then move to exercise 1.","response":"Section 10 is not a file you run. It is a self-check: five questions you answer from what you have read, with no code to execute. The idea is to predict before you run, which is the habit the whole sub-module is about.

Take the first one. In `widgets.rs`, `label` has a default body on lines 12 to 14, and `Checkbox` overrides it on lines 30 to 32. `Text` does not override it. So what does `part.label()` print for the `Text` box and for the `Checkbox` boxes, and why does the same default body give different results for different types?

Say your answer out loud, then run `widgets.rs` and see if it matches. What do you expect?","topic":"section 10 self-check"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"EXPLAIN","confusion":"","confusion_skill":"","note":"They asked why map does nothing until a consumer runs. Their guess (adapter returns a new iterator object holding the closure, consumer calls next) is essentially right. Confirm it and push them to see the laziness in iterators.rs lines 21-27, then to section 7 and adapters.rs.","response":"Yes, that is the whole of it. `map` does not run the closure; it builds a small struct that holds the closure and a reference to the iterator it wraps. That struct implements `Iterator`, so it has a `next` method. Nothing calls `next` until a consumer does, and `collect` is a consumer.

So the chain is just a stack of structs, each one holding the next. When `collect` asks for the first item, the request travels right to left through the stack, and the item comes back left to right. That is why the "map sees" lines in `iterators.rs` print only after line 26, the `collect` call.

Now look at `iterators.rs` lines 21 to 27. If you removed `.collect()` on line 26, what would the compiler warn you about, and why does that warning make sense given what you just said?","topic":"why adapters are lazy"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"TRY","confusion":"Believes `println!` of an adapter chain runs the closure, so `println!` is a consumer.","confusion_skill":"F5","entity_id":868,"note":"They restated the laziness error again. Had them run a one-line test: printing a Map shows no 'map sees' lines. Next turn ask what actually pulled items.","response":"Good question, and the answer is in the file in front of you. In `iterators.rs`, line 21-24 builds a `map` adapter and line 25 prints a plain string. The closure body on line 22 prints "map sees".

So add one line right after line 25, before the `collect` on line 26:

```rust
println!("{:?}", doubled);
```

Run it and tell me: do you see the "map sees" lines, or just a `Map { .. }` value?","suggestion":"After line 25, add `println!("{:?}", doubled);` before the collect on line 26, then run.","topic":"println! is not a consumer"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks a closure stored in a `Box<dyn Fn>` can borrow a local `&str` and still be `'static`.","confusion_skill":"F4","highlights":[{"entity_id":906,"end_line":20,"path":"","start_line":16},{"entity_id":906,"end_line":59,"path":"","start_line":52}],"note":"They pasted ex2_rules.rs code but the run output shows tests named keeps_the_same_buffer, basic, empty, which are not the tests in ex2_rules.rs (single_rules, rule_outlives_the_string_it_was_made_from, rules_of_different_closure_types_in_one_vec). So the run did not come from the file they pasted. Pointed at without() and the rule_outlives test. Next: have them run the real ex2_rules.rs and read the lifetime error, then fix without() by owning the word.","response":"Your pasted code is not what ran. The output shows tests called `keeps_the_same_buffer`, `basic`, `empty` - none of those exist in `ex2_rules.rs`. The real tests are `single_rules`, `rule_outlives_the_string_it_was_made_from` and `rules_of_different_closure_types_in_one_vec`. Run the real file again and read the output.

Now look at your `without` on lines 16 to 20 of `ex2_rules.rs`. It takes `word: &str` and hands that reference straight to the closure. The closure is stored in a `Box<dyn Fn>`, and in a function signature `Box<dyn Fn>` means `Box<dyn Fn + 'static>`. So what does the closure have to own? And what does `word` own at the moment `without` returns?

Then look at the test on lines 52 to 59. `temp` is dropped at the closing brace, but the rule is used after that. What does that tell you about what the closure must hold onto?","topic":"boxed closures must own"}}

