{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks `without` needs its own copy of the word, but the closure still captures the `&str` parameter directly instead of owning a copy.","confusion_skill":"F4","highlights":[{"entity_id":906,"end_line":20,"path":"","start_line":18},{"entity_id":906,"end_line":59,"path":"","start_line":52}],"note":"They correctly identified that `without` needs its own copy, but the code still borrows `word`. Next: have them add `let word = word.to_string();` and `move`, then run. Also check `max_len` and `one_of` for `move` (usize is Copy, Vec is moved in).","response":"You are right that `without` needs its own copy, but look at line 19: the closure still names `word`, which is the `&str` parameter. `move` moves the reference into the closure, not the string it points at. So the box still holds a borrow.

Two edits on line 19:

```rust
fn without(word: &str) -> Rule {
    let word = word.to_string();
    Box::new(move |text| !text.contains(word.as_str()))
}
```

`to_string()` gives the closure its own owned copy, and `move` moves that copy in. Now the box is `'static`.

While you are there, look at line 12 and line 23. Does `max_len` need `move`? Does `one_of`? Make your call, run the tests, and tell me what you see.","topic":"owning captures in boxed closures"}}

