{"move_object":{"move":"POINT","confusion":"Thinks `while let` does a cleanup/destructuring step on the last matched value, and that `top` is a borrow of the last element rather than a copy.","confusion_skill":"C5","highlights":[{"entity_id":786,"end_line":26,"path":"","start_line":23}],"note":"They invented a cleanup/destructuring step for while let and think `top` is a borrow of the last element. Pointed at places.rs 23-26 and asked what type `top` is and where it comes from. Next turn: if they say `top: i32` (a copy), confirm and move to the `matches!` question; if they still say borrow, explain that `Some(top)` binds the inner value by value and the loop body owns it.","response":"Ek cheez pehle clear kar lein: `while let` koi cleanup ya destructuring step nahi karta. Uska rule sirf do line ka hai: har iteration ke shuru me pattern ko test karo, match hua to body chalao, fail hua to loop wahin khatam. Koi extra borrow, koi extra step nahi.

Ab `top` ka type dekho. `stack.pop()` `Option<i32>` deta hai, aur pattern `Some(top)` hai. Toh `top` ka type kya hai, aur woh value kahan se aati hai? `Some` ke andar jo value hai, woh `top` me kaise pahunchti hai, copy ya borrow?

`matches!` ka sawaal uske baad, ek baar yeh clear ho jaaye.","topic":"while let: no cleanup"}}

{"move_object":{"move":"TRY","confusion":"Believes a closure that mutates a captured counter is still `Fn` because `move` gives ownership; `move` does not change the trait a closure implements.","confusion_skill":"F4","entity_id":903,"note":"They think `move` makes a mutating closure `Fn`. Next: check they can say the closure implements `FnMut` (and `FnOnce`), so `Box<dyn Fn>` rejects it, and that `move` only moves the binding, not the mutability of the capture.","response":"`move` moves the binding, not the mutability of what is captured. A closure that writes to a captured `seen` is `FnMut`, and `FnMut` is not `Fn`. So `Box<dyn Fn(&str) -> bool>` will not accept it.

Try it. In `boxed_fn.rs`, replace line 21 with this:

```rust
let mut seen = 0;
rules.push(Box::new(move |s| { seen += 1; s.len() <= max_len }));
```

Run it. Which error do you get, and which trait does the compiler say the closure implements?","suggestion":"In boxed_fn.rs, replace line 21 with: let mut seen = 0; rules.push(Box::new(move |s| { seen += 1; s.len() <= max_len })); then run and read the error.","topic":"Fn vs FnMut on a mutating closure"}}

{"move_object": {"move": "PROBE", "asked": "Why is India's August accrual understated, and why did three months of exact parity against the old program not catch it, given the rewrite's August output summary and the two Accounting Flexfield structures.", "last_stage_performance": "Once the structures were read out, the candidate got there. They named the package's hard-coded segment and the 5000 to 7999 filter, said the filter is applied to the wrong segment for India, and correctly identified the India values in the summary as cost centres rather than accounts. They explained parity passed because the rewrite matched the old program's logic, and they framed the three months as a control failure, not just a code bug. They proposed deriving the natural account segment from the ledger's flexfield structure and selecting by account type rather than a number range. They did not quantify the missing accrual, did not say the June to August GL figures are already wrong and must be raised with finance, and did not mention the natural account column in the output needing the same derivation or a per chart of accounts test case in the regression pack. Needed the structures read out before they could reason about them.", "note": "Moving to the DBA change. Want to see whether they judge the DBA's change against the deadline and EBS standards, and what they would need to see before either change goes to production.", "probe_id": 1, "response": "That is the one, and you have the shape of the fix right. Let us leave the flexfield there and move to the last thing.

The DBA team has a change they want in production on Tuesday 29 September, the night before the 30 September close. The rewrite has not been through UAT yet. Here is what they are proposing: they will add an index on the code combination column the package filters on, and they will change the filter to use the account type column instead of the number range. They say it is a small change, it is safe, and it will get India out of the red before close.

What do you tell the DBA team, and what would you need to see before either change goes to production?"}}

{"move_object": {"move": "PROBE", "asked": "Given the tkprof sorted by elapsed time, what does it tell you and what would you change?", "last_stage_performance": "Strong on the trace read: named the 847,000 executions of the AP invoice distribution aggregate as the dominant cost, the 847,000 GL_CODE_COMBINATIONS lookups, and the secondary INSERT, and correctly read the MO_GLOB_ORG_ACCESS_TMP reference as the org access predicate paid per execution. Proposed the right shape of fix: pre-aggregate invoiced quantity once by receipt, join GL_CODE_COMBINATIONS instead of looking it up, and rewrite the procedure set-based with a bulk insert. Did not quote the per-execution cost or the total elapsed for the aggregate, and did not tie the 1.4 million executions to India's volume against the pre-June baseline.", "note": "Moving to the flexfield and multi-org defect. Want to see whether they can explain why India's August accrual is low when the rewrite matches the old program line for line, and whether they connect it to the SEGMENT3 expense-account filter and the two Accounting Flexfield structures.", "probe_id": 1, "response": "That is the read I wanted: the cost is in the executions, not in any one statement, and you have named the two per-row lookups and the org access predicate in the plan. Let us leave the trace there.

