{"move_object":{"move":"QUESTION_DONE","note":"Closing the question. The candidate reasoned from the wiring and the README rather than from the numbers and the capture, and never reached the level-triggered re-fire mechanism or the shared-line read without a status check.","question_summary":"Mid-level candidate on a Linux ADC driver with a shared level-triggered RDY line. They correctly identified the shared-line design and the level-triggered re-fire, and proposed draining both ADCs in the threaded handler. They did not read the evidence: 243,963 interrupts in 10 s for 2,000 conversions, 82 and 162 hard-handler entries per conversion, repeated identical bytes and reads after RDY is already high, and the fact that the line is requested without IRQF_ONESHOT. They also did not mention the shared-line read without a status check, the spurious-IRQ detector, or the NULL primary handler comment.","report_notes":"Three stages, all on the same driver. Stage 1 (boot-time splat): correctly placed qadc_hardirq in hard interrupt context and gave the right shape of fix, moving the watchdog kick to the threaded handler. But they never named the actual sleeping call: the trace shows qadc_hardirq going through qadc_wdog_period into mutex_lock_nested on st->cfg_lock at mutex.c:585, and they guessed mod_timer instead, which is in fact legal in hardirq. They read no specific evidence out of the log: no preempt_count 10001, no irqs_disabled 128, no swapper/0, and they did not mention the second report at all, the inconsistent HARDIRQ-ON-W to IN-HARDIRQ-W state on cfg_lock registered at qadc_probe, nor the lock(); <Interrupt>; lock() deadlock scenario. The production-kernel answer was generic hand waving about latency spikes rather than the concrete case: contention with odr_store or the reset work, which holds cfg_lock across two I2C writes and usleep_range, causing a sleep in interrupt context and a hang on CPU0. No prompting was given; the answer was reasoned from first principles about top halves rather than from the files.

Stage 2 (second lockdep report on st->lock): strong and largely self-directed. They read the report correctly, the lock registered from hard interrupt context in qadc_hardirq versus the same lock taken with interrupts enabled from latest_show on the sysfs read path, and described the same-CPU self-deadlock accurately, with the handler unable to make progress and the interrupted task unable to release. They enumerated all the users: qadc_hardirq plain spin_lock, qadc_irq_thread already irqsave, qadc_wdog_expired needing irqsave because the timer callback can be interrupted by the hard IRQ on the same CPU, latest_show needing irqsave, and stats_show. Gaps: they did not use the evidence that would explain why units hang only rarely, namely that the GPIO bank interrupt is serviced on CPU0 while this particular report came from cat on CPU1, so lockdep flagged the pattern without an actual hang. They hedged on stats_show, saying it needs irqsave if it takes the lock, rather than noticing it takes no lock at all and what that costs for a 64-bit counter on a 32-bit ARM. They did not raise the option of taking st->lock out of the hard handler entirely. No hints were needed.

Stage 3 (production interrupt storm): the weakest stage. They reasoned from the wiring and the README rather than from the files. They correctly identified the shared open-drain line, the level-triggered request, and the idea that the handler does not drain both devices, and proposed looping over both clients in the threaded handler. But they never read the numbers: line 63 went from 44,792,380 to 45,036,343, 243,963 interrupts in 10 s for 2,000 conversions, about 122 per conversion, all on CPU0. They never read the capture: RDY low 317.2 us with 82 hard-handler entries, 621.9 us with 162, one entry every 3.83 us, and the repeated identical bytes and reads after RDY is already high. They did not reach the mechanism: the line is requested without IRQF_ONESHOT, so the line is re-fired until a thread has done the I2C read, and the shared line wakes both threads, each of which reads without checking STATUS and always returns IRQ_HANDLED, so each driver reads its ADC on the other ADC's conversions too. They did not mention the comment about the kernel refusing a NULL primary handler without ONESHOT, nor the spurious-IRQ detector. They also did not mention that the 0x49 conversion keeps the line low twice as long because 0x48's thread reads first and the bus serialises. No hints were given; the answer was a plausible but generic shared-IRQ story rather than one grounded in the evidence.","response":"That is a fair read of the wiring, and draining both clients in the thread is the right instinct. What I would push on is the evidence: the numbers in irq-check.txt and the two captures in rdy-capture.png. The interrupt count on line 63 and the number of conversions in 10 s, and what the capture shows about how long RDY stays low and how often the hard handler is entered. We are out of time, so let us stop there. Thanks for walking through it with me.","title":"Fix interrupt and locking bugs in a Linux ADC driver","topic":"Linux interrupt handling: hard and threaded handlers, IRQF_ONESHOT, shared level-triggered lines"}}

